  "body": "The following algorithms operate on a document, but do not explicitly take the document as an argument. Instead they implicitly operate on \"the Document\" or similar.\r\n\r\n- [ ] [unload a document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#unload-a-document)\r\n- [ ] [unloading a document cleanup steps](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#unloading-document-cleanup-steps)\r\n- [ ] [abort a document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#abort-a-document)\r\n- [ ] [discard a document](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/window-object.html#discard-a-document)\r\n\r\nCompare to [prompt to unload](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsing-the-web.html#prompt-to-unload-a-document), which does the right thing.\r\n\r\nFixing this bug would involve, for each of these algorithms:\r\n\r\n- Adding that argument to the algorithm's argument list\r\n- Using _document_ instead of \"the `Document`\" or \"_this_ document\" throughout the algorithm.\r\n- <del>Updating all callers of the algorithm to explicitly pass a document to it.</del><ins>This seems unnecessary in fact; see below conversation</ins>\r\n\r\nI will tag this as \"good first issue\", but it is more difficult than other good first issues; it might be more of a \"good second issue\", or for someone who feels like they understand things pretty well.",
